NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"id":"concepts","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Concepts"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"ranger-is-a-headless-ui-library","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ranger is a \"headless\" UI library"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ranger is a headless utility, which means out of the box, it doesn't render or supply any actual UI elements. You are in charge of utilizing the state and callbacks of the hooks provided by this library to render your own table markup. "},{"type":"link","href":"https://www.merrickchristensen.com/articles/headless-user-interface-components/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Read this article to understand why Ranger is built this way"}]},{"type":"text","value":". If you don't want to, then here's a quick rundown anyway:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Separation of Concerns - Not that superficial kind you read about all the time. The real kind. Ranger as a library honestly has no business being in charge of your UI. The look, feel, and overall experience of your table is what makes your app or product great. The less Ranger gets in the way of that, the better!"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Maintenance - By removing the massive (and seemingly endless) API surface area required to support every UI use-case, Ranger can remain small, easy-to-use and simple to update/maintain."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Extensibility - UI presents countless edge cases for a library simply because it's a creative medium, and one where every developer does things differently. By not dictating UI concerns, Ranger empowers the developer to design and extend the UI based on their unique use-case."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"the-ranger-instance","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Ranger instance"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"At the heart of every Ranger is the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Ranger"},{"type":"text","value":" class. This class will provide everything you'll ever need to build a ranger and interact with its state. This includes, but is not limited to:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Value Range"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Snap Interpolation"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ticks (labels) generation"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"After reading about Ranger's concepts, you should:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"/ranger/latest/docs/framework/react/examples/basic","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Check Out Some Examples"}]}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"concepts","text":"Concepts","level":1},{"id":"ranger-is-a-headless-ui-library","text":"Ranger is a \"headless\" UI library","level":2},{"id":"the-ranger-instance","text":"The Ranger instance","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n# Concepts\n\n## Ranger is a \"headless\" UI library\n\nRanger is a headless utility, which means out of the box, it doesn't render or supply any actual UI elements. You are in charge of utilizing the state and callbacks of the hooks provided by this library to render your own table markup. [Read this article to understand why Ranger is built this way](https://www.merrickchristensen.com/articles/headless-user-interface-components/). If you don't want to, then here's a quick rundown anyway:\n\n- Separation of Concerns - Not that superficial kind you read about all the time. The real kind. Ranger as a library honestly has no business being in charge of your UI. The look, feel, and overall experience of your table is what makes your app or product great. The less Ranger gets in the way of that, the better!\n- Maintenance - By removing the massive (and seemingly endless) API surface area required to support every UI use-case, Ranger can remain small, easy-to-use and simple to update/maintain.\n- Extensibility - UI presents countless edge cases for a library simply because it's a creative medium, and one where every developer does things differently. By not dictating UI concerns, Ranger empowers the developer to design and extend the UI based on their unique use-case.\n\n## The Ranger instance\n\nAt the heart of every Ranger is the `Ranger` class. This class will provide everything you'll ever need to build a ranger and interact with its state. This includes, but is not limited to:\n\n- Value Range\n- Snap Interpolation\n- Ticks (labels) generation\n\nAfter reading about Ranger's concepts, you should:\n\n- [Check Out Some Examples](/ranger/latest/docs/framework/react/examples/basic)\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nname: Concepts\nroute: /concepts\n---\n\n# Concepts\n\n## Ranger is a \"headless\" UI library\n\nRanger is a headless utility, which means out of the box, it doesn't render or supply any actual UI elements. You are in charge of utilizing the state and callbacks of the hooks provided by this library to render your own table markup. [Read this article to understand why Ranger is built this way](https://www.merrickchristensen.com/articles/headless-user-interface-components/). If you don't want to, then here's a quick rundown anyway:\n\n- Separation of Concerns - Not that superficial kind you read about all the time. The real kind. Ranger as a library honestly has no business being in charge of your UI. The look, feel, and overall experience of your table is what makes your app or product great. The less Ranger gets in the way of that, the better!\n- Maintenance - By removing the massive (and seemingly endless) API surface area required to support every UI use-case, Ranger can remain small, easy-to-use and simple to update/maintain.\n- Extensibility - UI presents countless edge cases for a library simply because it's a creative medium, and one where every developer does things differently. By not dictating UI concerns, Ranger empowers the developer to design and extend the UI based on their unique use-case.\n\n## The Ranger instance\n\nAt the heart of every Ranger is the `Ranger` class. This class will provide everything you'll ever need to build a ranger and interact with its state. This includes, but is not limited to:\n\n- Value Range\n- Snap Interpolation\n- Ticks (labels) generation\n\nAfter reading about Ranger's concepts, you should:\n\n- [Check Out Some Examples](/ranger/latest/docs/framework/react/examples/basic)\n"}
